WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.290
Speaker 1: It's great to have back on the
program today Dr. Isaac Bogot, an infectious

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:11.320
diseases physician and scientist based out
of the University of Toronto and Toronto General

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.340
Hospital.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:13.340
Great having you back again.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.059
Speaker 4: Great to see you again.

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:17.060
Speaker 1: So where to even start?

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:23.860
OK, I mean, over the last over the last month
or so, we've seen a global decline of about

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:27.800
30, 31, 32 percent and daily case average.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.710
The U.S. has seen a similar decline.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:33.970
Some countries are a little bit ahead of that,
some a little bit behind.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:35.290
What's the explanation here?

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.790
Is it vaccination getting to a critical point?

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:39.280
Is there still some seasonality?

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.470
We don't understand.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:44.170
Is it just inaccurate reporting and maybe
cases aren't really going down?

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:49.480
Speaker 4: Yeah, I think I think it's a lot
of the above, in fact, all of the above.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:56.000
So certainly in countries that have significant
vaccine access and vaccine rollout, that's

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.000
definitely helping.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Right.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.010
That's definitely keeping cases down or driving
cases down.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.390
And we can't ignore that.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:04.710
But of course, there's probably other factors
at play.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:09.250
We know that vaccine equity, global vaccine
equity is a major issue.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.640
There still are billions of people around
the world without access to these vaccines,

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.720
mainly in low income countries.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.540
And there are, you know, depending on where
you are in the world, there are very significant

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.850
outbreaks and epidemics of this.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:25.720
So globally, it's great to see those cases
go down.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.620
I think it's too soon to start having, you
know, mission accomplished banners up or anything

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:31.620
like that.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.510
We're still at a global level, far from the
end.

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:38.650
But certainly in countries with high vaccine
uptake, like the United States, like Canada,

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:42.890
like many European countries and the few countries
in Asia, yeah, we are closer to the end.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:47.600
We might be in the second or third period
rather than in the first period like some

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:48.600
other countries are.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:54.110
Speaker 1: So last summer of twenty twenty,
there was the correct belief that the fall

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:58.110
into winter in the Northern Hemisphere was
going to get pretty bad.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:01.280
At the time we were just starting to roll
out vaccines.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.710
There was very limited availability.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:06.420
The peak ended up being in the U.S.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:11.160
It was basically January 20th, right around
presidential inauguration day.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.800
In other countries, it was a little bit earlier,
a little bit later.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:21.030
And then we saw the sort of big decline and
then we got the Delta spike this time around

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:25.590
where vaccination is available in many of
these countries.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:30.350
And we're still not totally sure about the
seasonality match up that that sort of mirrors

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:33.060
the flu's seasonality.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.980
What do we think about this fall in winter
in the Northern Hemisphere?

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.980
Speaker 4: I think there's a couple of points.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.720
One is that when we talk about seasonality,
it might mean different things to different

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:43.720
people.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:49.930
And yes, I'm sure eventually this virus will
have a more seasonal component.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.450
Maybe it'll be more present in certain times
of year, like the colder months versus the

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:54.660
warmer months.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.200
And that's that's very likely to happen with
time.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.599
But when I'm talking about seasonality now,
I'm I'm oversimplifying it.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:07.000
But it kind of can be simplified in the sense
that whatever puts people indoors is is a

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.880
is a risk factor for greater transmission.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.930
Hey, it's cold outside and it's September.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.170
So kids are going back to school.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:16.810
Many people are going back to work in person.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.540
And we're not hanging out outdoors any as
much anymore because it's getting chilly.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.980
We're spending recreational time indoors.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.560
And that in and of itself at a population
level can certainly drive numbers up because

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.200
there's just innumerable opportunities for
this virus to be transmitted.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.569
So when we put that together and look at the
northern hemisphere, yes, it is getting cooler.

00:03:35.569 --> 00:03:38.430
People are going inside for more and more
reason.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:43.830
I think it's fair to say that we will see
peaks and valleys, but probably peaks throughout

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:47.900
the cooler months and the fall in the winter
in the northern hemisphere.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.709
And, you know, sort of stating the obvious.

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:56.750
I think areas that have more widespread vaccine
uptake will have more blunted peak.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:01.989
So the amplitude of those peaks is really
under our control in countries like Canada,

00:04:01.989 --> 00:04:05.459
the United States, that are fortunate enough
to have access to those vaccines.

00:04:05.459 --> 00:04:09.129
You can vaccinate yourself and those around
you in your community.

00:04:09.129 --> 00:04:14.730
You can create safer indoor spaces as well
with masking, with ventilation, with crowd

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:16.160
control.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.139
Like there's a lot of steps we can take to
create safer indoor spaces at a population

00:04:20.139 --> 00:04:25.759
level as well, so that you reap the benefits
of of those vaccination campaigns plus those

00:04:25.759 --> 00:04:27.360
non pharmaceutical interventions.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:28.669
So short story long.

00:04:28.669 --> 00:04:34.139
Yeah, I mean, we'll probably have some peaks
and valleys throughout the fall, in the winter,

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:37.349
but how high those peaks go are under our
control.

00:04:37.349 --> 00:04:40.860
We have the tools to keep this under control
and so on.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:45.819
Speaker 1: Speaking of tools, there was a
period where it seemed as though every adult

00:04:45.819 --> 00:04:48.229
was headed towards a third shot.

00:04:48.229 --> 00:04:50.990
Originally, the word booster was used.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:56.110
But when it was when the framing was, no,
no, no, it may be a three shot regimen.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:59.270
The change of language was it's not a it's
not a booster.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.669
It's just part of what you're supposed to
do.

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:07.069
And then all of a sudden we started seeing
a change in tone to some degree, where at

00:05:07.069 --> 00:05:14.949
least in the U.S., the recommendation change
to only third shots for 65 plus immunocompromised

00:05:14.949 --> 00:05:20.580
preexisting conditions, which if you include
being overweight and smoking, is a lot of

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:21.629
people in the United States.

00:05:21.629 --> 00:05:22.629
Right.

00:05:22.629 --> 00:05:25.719
I mean, but let's let's put that aside just
for a second here.

00:05:25.719 --> 00:05:31.240
What's your sense of because there's the medical
question as to who would really benefit.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:35.800
There's a vaccine equity question as to should
people be getting third shots or is are those

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:39.729
better used as first shots in other parts
of the world?

00:05:39.729 --> 00:05:41.180
How should we be thinking about it?

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:46.979
Speaker 4: David, I love how you separate
those questions, because oftentimes that gets

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:51.770
confused and from a medical standpoint, like
this is a very valid medical question.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:54.310
Do people need a third dose of the vaccine?

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:58.870
And if we want to delve into that, I think
the answer is, like anything else, it's a

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:01.129
bit more complicated.

00:06:01.129 --> 00:06:07.830
I think there's good data to drive that immunocompromised
individuals, however we want to define it,

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:09.599
would benefit from a third dose of a vaccine.

00:06:09.599 --> 00:06:10.599
Right.

00:06:10.599 --> 00:06:13.680
They just don't mount the same degree of an
immune response as other people.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.569
And as you point out, this can be viewed as
you know, many people have had two doses of

00:06:17.569 --> 00:06:18.759
a three dose regimen.

00:06:18.759 --> 00:06:21.089
You need that third dose for better protection.

00:06:21.089 --> 00:06:24.249
We've known this for a million years, well
before covid-19.

00:06:24.249 --> 00:06:28.969
There's a reason we give immunocompromised
individuals double doses of, for example,

00:06:28.969 --> 00:06:33.240
the hepatitis B vaccine like this is no similar
to that.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:38.399
People who are on the older end of the spectrum
will probably benefit from a third dose of

00:06:38.399 --> 00:06:39.629
a vaccine in the United States.

00:06:39.629 --> 00:06:41.550
It was defined as sixty five and up.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:45.639
We could argue and debate about the data,
but that's not unreasonable, including those

00:06:45.639 --> 00:06:48.620
who live in Kongregate care settings like
long term care facilities.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:51.389
And again, we've known this long before covid-19
came around.

00:06:51.389 --> 00:06:57.259
There's a reason we give older cohorts the
higher dose influenza vaccine every year because

00:06:57.259 --> 00:07:01.819
we know they need an extra boost to mount
an immune response.

00:07:01.819 --> 00:07:07.069
But I got to say and, you know, when we're
you know, I'm sitting in Canada now when we

00:07:07.069 --> 00:07:11.189
were listening to the United States discuss
this, if we were in mid-August, we heard very

00:07:11.189 --> 00:07:15.449
senior political and public health leaders
say everyone's getting a booster shot at the

00:07:15.449 --> 00:07:16.449
six month mark.

00:07:16.449 --> 00:07:17.449
Oops.

00:07:17.449 --> 00:07:18.449
Pardon.

00:07:18.449 --> 00:07:19.929
Sorry, everyone is getting a booster at the
five month mark.

00:07:19.929 --> 00:07:20.929
Oops.

00:07:20.929 --> 00:07:23.129
No, no, we meant six months or eight months.

00:07:23.129 --> 00:07:26.719
And then they actually had to sit down and
you had the FDA discuss it.

00:07:26.719 --> 00:07:33.909
Then you had the vaccine experts discuss it,
like the process to get to this decision was

00:07:33.909 --> 00:07:39.919
very choppy and I think did a lot of damage
in terms of public trust in the vaccine efforts,

00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:42.819
especially when we know many unvaccinated
people currently.

00:07:42.819 --> 00:07:47.479
You know, we're for lack of a better word,
we're trying to win them over.

00:07:47.479 --> 00:07:51.610
We're trying to show and demonstrate how safe
and effective and reasonable it is to get

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:52.610
these vaccines.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:57.680
This didn't do a lot of good in getting to
that point, unfortunately.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:02.129
The other thing, too, is I don't know where
it really landed in the United States because

00:08:02.129 --> 00:08:08.580
it seems like, you know, if you're a smoker,
if you're obese, if you may work in a higher

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:12.649
risk setting, you can get it basically seems
like it's open season and anyone who wants

00:08:12.649 --> 00:08:14.509
to get a third dose can get a third dose.

00:08:14.509 --> 00:08:16.399
I don't think that's that's really appropriate.

00:08:16.399 --> 00:08:17.789
I'm not to blab on and on.

00:08:17.789 --> 00:08:21.550
But then let's factor in the equity issue.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:25.909
You've got hundreds of millions of people
that are eligible for a third dose when there's

00:08:25.909 --> 00:08:30.319
billions of people who haven't received the
first dose and hundreds or tens of millions

00:08:30.319 --> 00:08:33.010
of Americans that should be receiving a first
dose.

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:36.800
You want to get this under control, you get
first and second doses and as many people

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.840
as possible rather than third doses.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:43.010
Not to ignore that there are some people who
would really benefit from a third dose.

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:46.930
So to sum it all up, do we all need third
doses now?

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:48.170
No, we don't.

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:50.180
Do some of us need third doses now?

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:52.670
Yes, some of us do.

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:55.760
Will we all need a third dose at some point
in the future?

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:59.540
Yeah, we might all need a third dose at some
point in the future, but we don't all need

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:00.540
to third dose now.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:05.170
Speaker 1: So in terms of protection level,
which as you point out, can be in terms of

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:10.040
larger populations, the protection level is
lower for certain folks.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:13.440
And that's a factor in thinking about these
third doses.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:21.380
How should we be thinking now about how protected
one is today, given the difference between

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:27.020
the dominant Delta strain and what was the
original or live strain for which the vaccines

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:28.470
were calculated?

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:34.161
Because you hear you still got a mask if you're
vaccinated because you can still spread it.

00:09:34.161 --> 00:09:36.810
And then you hear, hey, actually you can.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:41.680
But if you're vaccinated and asymptomatic,
it's not super likely.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:45.110
But it's but it's possible and it makes it
makes it very difficult to do like a risk

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.140
assessment of any individual.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:53.780
So when you read 99, 98, 97 percent of the
cases and or at least hospitalizations are

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:56.800
in the unvaccinated, what does that tell us?

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:02.180
Speaker 4: Yeah, basically, you know, sometimes
people complain about shifting goalposts and

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:03.280
you're changing the rules.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:06.370
And the answer is yes, of course they're shifting
goalposts.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:08.810
Of course, you're changing the rules and adapting
policy.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:11.620
You have to because the virus has changed
as well.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:15.910
And the Delta variant is, of course, it's
the same virus, but it functions in a different

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:16.910
manner.

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:21.460
It's way more transmissible compared to the
original virus that emerged from Juhan.

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:24.200
So, yes, you have to have these shifting goalposts.

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:29.660
The problem is communicating this and and
telegraphing uncertainty and communicating

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:31.880
that there will be changes over time.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:36.500
Some we can expect, some we not we might not
be able to expect so that people don't feel

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:38.830
like they're getting the rug pulled from underneath
them.

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:40.690
So that's that that's one important point.

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:41.690
The second point.

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:49.050
Yeah, I mean, Delta is much more transmissible,
but we do know that if you're vaccinated right

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:54.020
off the bat, Delta or not, especially even
with Delta, you still reduce the risk of getting

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:55.130
covid-19.

00:10:55.130 --> 00:11:00.840
You reduce the risk of getting infected, but
you greatly reduce your risk of getting really

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:04.560
sick, like landing in hospital, needing an
ICU or dying.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:09.500
And there's just overwhelming data from multiple
countries demonstrating all this.

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:15.530
The second point is you can still get infected
with Delta even if you're vaccinated.

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:16.530
We've known that.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:20.360
We've known that long before Delta even emerged
in the original clinical trials that were

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:23.010
published, there were still vaccinated people
that got infected.

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:24.470
Delta hadn't even emerged yet.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:26.410
So that's not new.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:33.110
But we do know now that if you are vaccinated
and infected, you're just much less likely

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:34.570
to transmit to other people.

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:37.470
Of course, you can still transmit, but the
probability is lower.

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:42.230
So, you know, for example, if you want to
create a safe indoor space, a restaurant,

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:47.900
a bar, a casino, a day care, wherever, the
more vaccinated people you have that indoor

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:52.680
space, you just reduce a the likelihood that
you're bringing covid-19 into that space.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:57.410
And B, if it is brought in the likelihood
that it's transmitted to other people, of

00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:00.340
course, that's one significant layer of protection.

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:02.430
But we have to think of this as layers of
protection.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:06.960
There's other layers you can take, masking,
ventilation, crowd control.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:10.710
And when you put all those layers together
and you add that to vaccines, you want to

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:13.250
get really fancy at rapid testing at the door.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:17.710
The more layers you have, the safer the indoor
space you have, the more layers you peel off.

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:20.050
You're just adding incremental levels of risk.

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:23.570
And you've got to find that sweet spot of
what's reasonable, what's convenient and what's

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:25.450
necessary for that particular environment.

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:31.040
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to touch on,
when do cases no longer matter?

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:39.160
In a very specific sense, I think it was with
H1N1 that at a certain point when it was determined

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:43.170
that it's no more deadly than the flu strain
of that year.

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:47.690
And correct me if it's not H1N1, but it was
a different thing that I'm referring to.

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:51.910
At a certain point, the testing stopped because
it didn't really matter whether you had the

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:53.130
flu or you had.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:56.640
Again, I think it was H1N1 because the protocol
was no different.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:58.090
It was no more deadly.

00:12:58.090 --> 00:13:01.630
The case numbers may have been interesting,
but they didn't really tell us anything about

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:05.530
the risk to an individual of getting really
sick from being out in the world.

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:08.550
Does it depend on the level of vaccination?

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:14.870
Does it depend on the maybe reduced severity
of coronavirus as we go forward, which may

00:13:14.870 --> 00:13:16.660
happen or it may not?

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:19.230
When do the case numbers no longer matter?

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:25.320
Speaker 4: So I think if you lined up 20 people
in the business, you'd probably get several

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:26.320
different answers.

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:31.350
The way I think about this, though, is I like
the case numbers, but it has to be put into

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:32.560
the appropriate context.

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:33.560
Right.

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:37.380
There's a lot of data hospitalizations, ICU
death cases vaccinated, percent positive,

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:39.370
blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:40.900
But context is important.

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:44.730
So you have to put the appropriate weight
and context to each data point.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:50.420
But when the case numbers sort of start to
lower in the importance factor, I think it's

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:55.490
in my mind it's really when we start to see
this virus not impact our society as much

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:56.490
anymore.

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:57.490
What do you mean by that?

00:13:57.490 --> 00:14:02.770
It's not resulting in an overwhelming number
of hospitalizations or deaths or morbidity.

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:05.240
It's not preventing people from doing what
they do.

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:07.850
And a lot of that's a value judgment as well,
right?

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:10.410
There's a value judgment here.

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.750
It's kind of unfortunate and disgusting to
think about.

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:20.900
But at some point we're going to have to collectively
decide what is an acceptable number of deaths

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:23.010
per year for covid-19.

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:25.220
Not nice to think about, but we have to figure
that out.

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:29.510
Like in Canada, for example, influenza kills
thirty five hundred people per year.

00:14:29.510 --> 00:14:30.730
Awful, terrible.

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:31.770
Get your flu shot.

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:34.230
All of them are tragic.

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:39.130
But as a country, we've sort of come to terms
with facts about how many we see per year.

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:46.620
We have to come to terms with how many covid-19
deaths or cases or what is quote unquote acceptable.

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:47.850
And again, some will say zero.

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:51.640
I don't think that's practical.

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:53.200
Of course, there's the letter rip crowd.

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:55.910
I don't think that's safe or ethical as well.

00:14:55.910 --> 00:15:00.340
But eventually enough people have immunity
through vaccination or recovery from infection

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:06.090
that it just doesn't pose a burden on the
country or the region as much as it used to.

00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:07.880
You're not going to overwhelm your your health
care system.

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:12.790
You can go to restaurants and bars and live
your life, but the virus isn't going away.

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:14.690
So it's going to be a bit of a value judgment.

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:17.350
That's a bit of a long winded way of answering
that question.

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:23.200
But at some point, as long as it when it's
when it impacts the country or the region

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:27.570
less by not overwhelming the health care system
and not causing.

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:31.650
Meaningful morbidity and mortality, that's
probably when the case counts, don't matter.

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:36.540
Speaker 1: Yeah, and it will still be on a
different call for different individuals also

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:41.620
based on their their level of risk and their
willingness to accept risk as well.

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:45.860
So there's no it's a it's a complex answer
because it's a complex question, I guess,

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:47.560
is the way we would say it.

00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:52.030
We've been speaking with Dr. Isaac Bogarde,
infectious diseases physician and scientist

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:55.720
based at the University of Toronto and Toronto
General Hospital.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:57.290
Always great catching up with you.

